title: Autosave and lasting saves
intro: An Autosave saves the game by itself, a Continue button picks it up, a saved game keeps the scripts' own model, and saved games load in the next version of the game.

project: Empty | Autosave

chapter: Saving by itself | Autosave
create: Empty
rename: Autosave
add: Autosave
explain: field:Autosave.OnArrive | On Arrive | An Autosave saves the game in its own slot as its scene starts: a checkpoint as the player arrives, though not as a saved game is loaded.
set: Autosave.EveryMinutes = 1
explain: field:Autosave.EveryMinutes | Every Minutes | Then every so many minutes of play. They're game time, so they don't pass while the game is paused.
explain: field:Autosave.OnQuit | On Quit | And as the game closes, or as Play stops here.
explain: field:Autosave.Setting | Setting | Setting names a switch in the save data, a toggle on the options screen, that turns it off for players who'd rather it didn't.
say: Put one in each scene that's played, and none on the title screen.

chapter: The Auto slot | Continue picks it up
create: Empty
rename: Auto Slot
add: SaveSlot
set: SaveSlot.Slot = Auto
explain: field:SaveSlot.Slot | Slot | A Save Slot shows what's saved in the Auto slot.
play
wait: 2
say: As the scene started it saved, so the slot shows the game's picture and the time. A Continue button loads the game saved last, so it picks the autosave up.
stop

chapter: Scripts keep their model | What scripts keep
write: Scripts/Workshop.cs | autosave/Workshop.cs
create: Text
rename: Workshop
add: Workshop
add: ScreenAnchor
code: Assets/Scripts/Workshop.cs | Workshop.cs
say: A game's model is mostly in its scripts' fields. This workshop keeps its orders as plain objects in a private list, and the one it's making in another field: the same object. S saves, X ships everything, L loads.
hidecode
play
tap: Space
tap: Space
tap: Space
wait: 2
expectvalue: Workshop.Workshop.Orders = 3
expectvalue: Workshop.Workshop.Making = 1
say: Three orders, and the first is under way. Save the game.
tap: S
wait: 1
tap: X
wait: 0.5
expectvalue: Workshop.Workshop.Orders = 0
say: The game goes on, and every order is shipped. Now load the saved game.
tap: L
wait: 1.5
expectvalue: Workshop.Workshop.Orders = 3
expectvalue: Workshop.Workshop.Making = 1
say: The orders are back, and the first is still the one being made, carrying on from where it had got to. A saved game keeps scripts' private fields and the objects they hold. A coroutine isn't saved, so the script starts its work again in On Loaded, from its fields; Not Saved leaves a field out.
stop

chapter: Saves that outlast updates | Save Version
say: Saved games are read back by name: entities, their components and their properties, and scripts' fields. So the next version of the game loads them even though its scripts changed. A property added keeps its default, one removed is ignored, and a component that's gone is kept as saved, with a warning.
menu: Game > Save Version
prompt: 2
say: For bigger changes, Save Version numbers the game's saves. Each saved game keeps the version it was saved by, and a system of the game's own, a Save Upgrader, brings older ones up to date as they load.
